\title[Full pinning at degree seven]{Full pinning at degree seven:\\
the abelian splitting-type ladder, its three channels,\\
and primality as the exact sufficient-statistic condition}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the information carried by residue data about the splitting behaviour of
primes in cyclic number fields of prime degree, and about the splitting behaviour
of the two prime factors of a semiprime. Fix a prime degree $q$ and a prime
conductor $f \equiv 1 \pmod q$, and let $K_q \subset \Q(\zeta_f)$ be the cyclic
subfield of degree $q$, the fixed field of the group of $q$-th powers in
$(\Z/f)^\times$. We prove that the residue degree $T(p)$ of an unramified prime
takes only the values $1$ and $q$, with density exactly $1/q$ for the value $1$;
that the type entropy is $H(T) = \log_2 q - \frac{q-1}{q}\log_2(q-1)$,
\emph{independent of the conductor}; and that the Frobenius class pins $T(p)$
completely, so the channel attains its capacity. The degree-seven rung, realised
by the septic subfield of $\Q(\zeta_{29})$, is worked out in full: the splitting
criterion is $p \bmod 29 \in \{1,12,17,28\}$, the densities are $1/7$ and $6/7$,
and $H(T) = \log_2 7 - \frac67 \log_2 6 = 0.5916727\ldots$ bits, certified between
$0.5916$ and $0.5918$; orthogonally, the quartic character mod $29$ carries
exactly zero information about the septic type. For semiprimes we introduce three
channels with the residue of the product as observation: the type-pair channel
$\Ipair$, the split-count channel $\Isplit$ and the OR channel $G$. At every prime
degree the split count is a sufficient statistic, $\Ipair(q) = \Isplit(q)$, and an
exact identity $\Isplit(q) - G(q) = H(S) - H(O)$ yields data processing. At degree
seven $\Isplit(7) = \log_2 7 + \frac{1}{49}(30\log_2 5 - 78\log_2 3 - 78) =
0.1141053\ldots$ and $G(7) = \log_2 7 + \frac{1}{49}(30\log_2 5 - 66\log_2 3 -
13\log_2 13 - 54) = 0.0103060\ldots$, with $11\,G(7) < \Isplit(7)$. Finally we
settle the first composite degree: $\Ipair(4) = 5/4$ exactly while $\Isplit(4) =
\frac{19}{8} - \frac{21}{16}\log_2 3 = 0.2947367\ldots$, and we prove that the
split count induces the same partition of the exponent box as the full type pair
if and only if the degree is prime.
\end{abstract}

