\section{Introduction}

Let $K/\Q$ be an abelian number field and $p$ an unramified rational prime. The
splitting type of $p$ in $K$ is a global invariant, yet by class field theory it
is determined by the residue of $p$ modulo the conductor. Information theory
quantifies this reciprocity miracle: treat the splitting type $T$ as a random
variable over a uniformly random Frobenius class, treat the residue datum as an
observation $Y$, and measure $I(Y;T)$ in bits. Reciprocity says $I(Y;T) = H(T)$
when $Y$ is the full Frobenius class --- the channel is \emph{fully pinned}. Once
the question is perturbed --- coarsening the observation, replacing a prime by a
product of two primes, or coarsening the hidden variable to a count --- the mutual
information drops, and by how much becomes a well-posed arithmetic problem with
exact answers. This paper carries out that programme for the ladder of cyclic
fields indexed by prime degree, closing the degree-seven rung and settling the
first composite degree.

Two techniques recur. First, a \emph{partition principle}: counting entropy
depends only on the partition into fibres induced by a read-out, so equalities
between channels defined by syntactically unrelated read-outs can be proved with
no recoding map. Second, \emph{certified transcendental brackets by integer
comparison}: every numerical claim below is reduced to an inequality between whole
numbers, so nothing depends on floating-point evaluation.

\section{The model}

Let $f$ be prime, so $\mathrm{Gal}(\Q(\zeta_f)/\Q) \cong (\Z/f)^\times$ is cyclic
of order $f-1$. For $q \ge 2$ let $P_{f,q} := \{u^q : u \in (\Z/f)^\times\}$ be
the subgroup of $q$-th powers and $K_q$ its fixed field; when $q \mid f-1$ the
quotient $(\Z/f)^\times/P_{f,q}$ is cyclic of order $q$ and $K_q/\Q$ is cyclic of
degree $q$.

\begin{definition}[residue degree]
For $u \in (\Z/f)^\times$ put $T_{f,q}(u) := \ord(uP_{f,q})$, the order of the
class of $u$ in the quotient. For a prime $p \nmid f$ write $T(p) :=
T_{f,q}(p \bmod f)$; this is the residue degree of a prime of $K_q$ above $p$, and
$T(p) = 1$ means that $p$ splits completely.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:basic}
$T_{f,q}(u) = 1$ iff $u \in P_{f,q}$; $T_{f,q}(u) \mid q$ always; hence for prime
$q$ one has $T_{f,q}(u) \in \{1,q\}$ (\emph{prime-degree dichotomy}). Moreover
$T_{f,q}(uh) = T_{f,q}(u)$ for all $h \in P_{f,q}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Order one means trivial class. Since $u^q \in P_{f,q}$, the class order divides
$q$; for prime $q$ the divisors are $1$ and $q$. Multiplication by an element of
$P_{f,q}$ does not change the class.
\end{proof}

All entropies are in bits with respect to the uniform measure on a finite set
$S$. For $g : S \to B$,
\[
H_S(g) := -\sum_{b \in g(S)} \frac{|g^{-1}(b)|}{|S|}\log_2\frac{|g^{-1}(b)|}{|S|},
\qquad
H_S(g \mid k) := \sum_{c \in k(S)} \frac{|k^{-1}(c)|}{|S|}H_{k^{-1}(c)}(g),
\]
and $I_S(g;k) := H_S(g) - H_S(g \mid k)$. Write $h(N,m)$ for the entropy of a
two-cell partition of sizes $m$ and $N-m$; thus $h(N,m) = h_2(m/N)$ and
$h(cN,cm) = h(N,m)$.

\begin{lemma}[partition principle]\label{lem:partition}
If $g(x) = g(y) \iff g'(x) = g'(y)$ for all $x,y \in S$, then $H_S(g) = H_S(g')$.
If this equivalence holds merely for $x,y$ in a common fibre of a third read-out
$k$, then $H_S(g\mid k) = H_S(g'\mid k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The fibres of $g$ and of $g'$ through each point coincide, so the two multisets of
fibre cardinalities agree and the defining sums are termwise equal; the
conditional statement is the unconditional one applied inside each fibre of $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:aux}
(i) If $g$ takes exactly two values then $H_S(g) = h(|S|,|g^{-1}(v)|)$ for either
value $v$. (ii) If $k(x) = k(y)$ implies $g(x) = g(y)$ then $H_S(g\mid k) = 0$ and
$I_S(g;k) = H_S(g)$. (iii) $H_S(\varphi \circ g) \le H_S(g)$ for every $\varphi$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The degree-seven rung: conductor $29$}

Here $q = 7$, $f = 29$, $(\Z/29)^\times \cong C_{28}$ and $C_{28}/C_4 \cong C_7$.

\begin{theorem}[two faces of the order-four subgroup]
For $u \in (\Z/29)^\times$ one has $u \in P_{29,7} \iff u^4 = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $u = v^7$ then $u^4 = v^{28} = 1$. Conversely if $u^4 = 1$ then
$(u^3)^7 = u^{21} = (u^4)^5u = u$.
\end{proof}

Solving $x^4 = 1$ in $\Z/29$ gives $x \in \{1,12,17,28\}$.

\begin{theorem}[degree-seven splitting criterion]\label{thm:crit}
A prime $p \ne 29$ splits completely in the septic subfield of $\Q(\zeta_{29})$
--- equivalently $\dlog(p) \equiv 0 \pmod 7$ --- if and only if
$p \bmod 29 \in \{1,12,17,28\}$; otherwise $T(p) = 7$.
\end{theorem}

Thus $41 \equiv 12$ splits completely while $2$ is inert of degree seven. Exactly
four of the $28$ classes split: densities $1/7$ and $6/7$.

\begin{theorem}[type entropy]\label{thm:HT}
$H(T) = h(28,4) = \log_2 7 - \frac67\log_2 6 = 0.5916727\ldots$ bits; equivalently
$7H(T) = \log_2(7^7/6^6)$; and $0.5916 < H(T) < 0.5918$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$T$ is binary by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}, so $H(T) = h(28,4) = h(7,1)$ by scale
invariance. The bracket is equivalent to the integer inequalities
$2^{8283}\cdot 6^{12000} < 7^{14000} < 2^{8284}\cdot 6^{12000}$, obtained by
raising $7^7/6^6$ to the power $2000$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[full pinning at degree seven]
Let $Q(u) := u\{1,12,17,28\}$ be the coset of $u$ modulo the seventh powers, a
datum with only seven values. Then $H(T \mid Q) = 0$ and $I(Q;T) = H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $Q(u) = Q(v)$ then $v = uw$ with $w^4 = 1$, hence $w \in P_{29,7}$, hence
$T(v) = T(u)$ since $T$ is a class function. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:aux}(ii).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[orthogonality of the quartic character]
In the exponent model, where $a$ runs uniformly over $\Z/28$, the septic type is
$T(a) = 1$ if $7 \mid a$ and $7$ otherwise, and $\chi_4(a) := a \bmod 4$, one has
$I(\chi_4;T) = 0$. In particular the quadratic residue symbol mod $29$ says
nothing about splitting in the septic subfield.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each of the four fibres of $\chi_4$ has seven elements, exactly one of which is
divisible by $7$; so every fibre has type entropy $h(7,1) = H(T)$. Structurally
this is the decomposition $C_{28} \cong C_4 \times C_7$ with $\gcd(4,7)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[semiprime split-count law]\label{thm:bin}
Let $(a,b)$ be uniform in $(\Z/7)^2$ and let $S$ be the number of coordinates
equal to $0$. Then of the $49$ pairs exactly $36$ have $S=0$, $12$ have $S=1$ and
$1$ has $S=2$; that is, $S \sim \mathrm{Bin}(2,1/7)$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\section{Conductor-freeness}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cyclic}
In a finite cyclic group $G = \langle g\rangle$ the $q$-th powers form the
subgroup $\langle g^q\rangle$, of order $|G|/\gcd(|G|,q)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[splitting density]
For prime $f$ and $q \mid f-1$, exactly $(f-1)/q$ Frobenius classes split
completely in the degree-$q$ subfield of $\Q(\zeta_f)$: the density is $1/q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[conductor-free entropy]\label{thm:cf}
For primes $f,q$ with $q \mid f-1$,
\[
H(T) = h\big(f-1,\tfrac{f-1}{q}\big) = h(q,1) = \log_2 q - \frac{q-1}{q}\log_2(q-1),
\]
independent of $f$. Two admissible conductors give literally the same entropy.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[universal full pinning]
For every modulus $f$ and every degree $q$, the Frobenius class in
$(\Z/f)^\times/P_{f,q}$ determines the residue degree, so $H(T\mid\text{class}) =
0$; under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:cf} this gives
$I(\text{class};T) = H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
The degree-seven rungs of conductors $29$ and $43$ carry exactly the same
entropy, and conductor $43$ satisfies the same bracket $0.5916 < H(T) < 0.5918$.
The rungs $(q,f) = (11,23)$, $(3,7)$, $(5,11)$ are further instances.
\end{corollary}

Numerically the ladder reads $H(T) = 1,\,0.9183,\,0.7219,\,0.5917,\,0.4395$ at
$q = 2,3,5,7,11$, decaying like $(\log_2 q)/q$.

\section{Three channels of a semiprime}

Fix $n \ge 2$. In discrete-logarithm coordinates a prime factor is an exponent
$a \in \Z/n$ with residue degree $\tau_n(a) := n/\gcd(n,a)$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{exponent box} is $B_n := \{(a,b) : 0 \le a,b < n\}$ with the uniform
measure. On it define the \emph{type pair}
$\Theta_n(a,b) := (\min(\tau_n(a),\tau_n(b)),\max(\tau_n(a),\tau_n(b)))$, the
\emph{split count} $S := \#\{\text{coordinates of }\Theta_n\text{ equal to }1\}
\in \{0,1,2\}$, the \emph{OR read-out} $O := \min(S,1)$, and the \emph{product
residue} $R_n(a,b) := (a+b) \bmod n$. Set
\[
\Ipair(n) := I_{B_n}(\Theta_n;R_n),\quad
\Isplit(n) := I_{B_n}(S;R_n),\quad
G(n) := I_{B_n}(O;R_n).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:typeprime}
For prime $q$ and $0\le a,b<q$: $\Theta_q(a,b) = (1,1)$ if $a=b=0$, $(1,q)$ if
exactly one of $a,b$ vanishes, and $(q,q)$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[sufficiency at prime degree]\label{thm:suff}
For prime $q$ the split count determines the type pair, and consequently
$\Ipair(q) = \Isplit(q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:typeprime} the split count equals $2,1,0$ exactly in the three
listed cases, so $S$ and $\Theta_q$ induce the same partition of $B_q$, with cells
of sizes $1$, $2(q-1)$, $(q-1)^2$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:partition}
unconditionally and inside each fibre of $R_q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[fibre structure]\label{lem:fibre}
For prime $q$ and $r \in \Z/q$, the fibre $R_q^{-1}(r)$ has $q$ elements. If
$r=0$ it contains one point with $S=2$ and $q-1$ points with $S=0$; if $r \ne 0$
it contains two points with $S=1$ and $q-2$ with $S=0$. In particular each fibre
carries at most one non-zero value of $S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:cond}
$H(S\mid R_q) = \frac1q h(q,1) + \frac{q-1}{q}h(q,2) = H(O \mid R_q)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[exact channel difference and data processing]
For prime $q$, $\Isplit(q) - G(q) = H(S) - H(O) \ge 0$; in particular
$G(q) \le \Isplit(q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Subtract the two mutual informations and cancel the conditional entropies by
Corollary~\ref{cor:cond}; non-negativity is Lemma~\ref{lem:aux}(iii) applied to
$O = \min(\cdot,1)\circ S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[closed forms at prime degree]\label{thm:closed}
For prime $q$, writing $H(c_0,c_1,c_2)$ for the entropy of the distribution
proportional to $(c_0,c_1,c_2)$,
\[
\Isplit(q) = H\big((q-1)^2,2(q-1),1\big) - \Big[\tfrac1q h(q,1) + \tfrac{q-1}{q}h(q,2)\Big],
\]
\[
G(q) = h\big(q^2,2q-1\big) - \Big[\tfrac1q h(q,1) + \tfrac{q-1}{q}h(q,2)\Big].
\]
\end{theorem}

\section{The degree-seven values}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:isplit7}
$\Isplit(7) = \log_2 7 + \dfrac{30\log_2 5 - 78\log_2 3 - 78}{49} = 0.1141053\ldots$,
and $0.1140 < \Isplit(7) < 0.1142$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Insert $q=7$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}: the split-count cells are $(36,12,1)$
out of $49$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:bin}, so the unconditional entropy is
$\log_2 49 - \frac{1}{49}(36\log_2 36 + 12\log_2 12)$, and the conditional term is
$\frac17 h(7,1) + \frac67 h(7,2)$. Expanding $\log_2 36 = 2+2\log_2 3$,
$\log_2 12 = 2+\log_2 3$, $\log_2 6 = 1+\log_2 3$, $\log_2 49 = 2\log_2 7$ and
$h(7,2) = \log_2 7 - \frac27 - \frac57\log_2 5$ gives the stated combination. The
bracket is equivalent to
$2^{16718}\cdot 3^{15600} < 7^{9800}\cdot 5^{6000} < 2^{16719}\cdot 3^{15600}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ior7}
$G(7) = \log_2 7 + \dfrac{30\log_2 5 - 66\log_2 3 - 13\log_2 13 - 54}{49} =
0.0103060\ldots$, and $0.01027 < G(7) < 0.01035$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
$11\,G(7) < \Isplit(7)$: collapsing the count $\{0,1,2\}$ to the single bit ``at
least one factor splits'' destroys more than ninety percent of the channel.
Moreover $\Isplit(11) = 0.051897\ldots < \Isplit(7)$, so the ladder is strictly
decreasing between the two rungs.
\end{corollary}

\section{Numerical audit}

Two numerical anchors were carried into this rung; closed forms settle both.

\emph{The anchor $0.0103$ is the OR channel (confirmed).} By
Theorem~\ref{thm:ior7}, $|G(7) - 0.0103| < 5\times 10^{-5}$.

\emph{The anchor $0.1161$ for the split-count channel is falsified.} By
Theorem~\ref{thm:isplit7}, $\Isplit(7) < 0.1161$ and $0.1161 - \Isplit(7) >
0.0018$, more than eighteen times the width of the certified bracket; the true
value is $0.1141053\ldots$.

\emph{A mislabelled rung.} A figure $0.116$ recorded elsewhere as the
degree-eleven split-count channel satisfies $|\Isplit(7) - 0.116| = 0.00190$
against $|\Isplit(11) - 0.116| = 0.06410$: it is more than thirty times closer to
the degree-seven rung.

The methodological lesson is that every quantity here is a finite combination of
logarithms of small integers and therefore admits an exactly certified bracket; a
$0.002$-bit discrepancy is invisible to sampling but fatal to a claimed identity.

\section{Composite degree}

\begin{theorem}[primality is the sufficient-statistic condition]\label{thm:iff}
Let $n \ge 2$. The implication $S(x) = S(y) \Rightarrow \Theta_n(x) = \Theta_n(y)$
holds for all $x,y \in B_n$ if and only if $n$ is prime.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For prime $n$ this is Theorem~\ref{thm:suff}. If $n = de$ with $1 < d < n$ and
$e \ge 2$, then $\tau_n(d) = e$ and $\tau_n(1) = n$, so $x = (d,d)$ and $y=(1,1)$
both have split count $0$ while $\Theta_n(x) = (e,e) \ne (n,n) = \Theta_n(y)$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is that the split count records only \emph{how many} trivial residue
degrees occur and forgets \emph{which} non-trivial degrees occur; that datum is
empty exactly when the divisor lattice of $\Z/n$ has a single non-trivial level.

At $n = 4$ the type pair takes six values with cell sizes
$(1,1)\!:\!1$, $(1,2)\!:\!2$, $(1,4)\!:\!4$, $(2,2)\!:\!1$, $(2,4)\!:\!4$,
$(4,4)\!:\!4$ out of $16$; the split count has cells $(9,6,1)$; the product
residue has four fibres of size four.

\begin{theorem}[entropies at degree four]
$H(\Theta_4) = \frac{19}{8}$, $H(\Theta_4 \mid R_4) = \frac98$,
$H(S) = \frac{29}{8} - \frac32\log_2 3$, and
$H(S \mid R_4) = \frac54 - \frac3{16}\log_2 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[the two channels at degree four]
$\Ipair(4) = \frac{19}{8} - \frac98 = \frac54$ and
$\Isplit(4) = \frac{19}{8} - \frac{21}{16}\log_2 3 = 0.2947367\ldots$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[cap behaviour and strict loss]
$\Ipair(4) = \frac54 > 1$ while $\Isplit(4) < 0.3 < 1$; hence
$\Isplit(4) < \frac14 \Ipair(4)$, so the projection to the split count destroys
more than three quarters of the channel. The witness for
$\Isplit(4) < 1$ is the integer bound $2^{22} < 3^{21}$, giving
$\log_2 3 > 22/21$ and $\Isplit(4) < \frac{19}{8} - \frac{11}{8} = 1$; the sharper
$0.3$ bound follows from $2^{3325} < 3^{2098}$.
\end{theorem}

Two features deserve emphasis: $\Ipair(4) = 5/4$ is rational --- unusual in a
family whose values are typically irrational combinations of logarithms --- and it
exceeds the one-bit cap that any binary read-out obeys, while the split count
restores that cap.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Splitting law of a prime rung.} Given a prime conductor $f$, a degree
$q \mid f-1$ and a prime $p \nmid f$: compute $P_{f,q} = \{u^q \bmod f\}$ in one
pass ($O(f\log q)$ modular multiplications), then return $1$ if $p \bmod f \in
P_{f,q}$ and $\ord(pP_{f,q})$ otherwise. For prime $q$ the test is Euler's
criterion $p^{(f-1)/q} \equiv 1 \pmod f$, costing $O(\log f)$ multiplications.

\textbf{B. Exact channel evaluation.} Enumerate the $n^2$ points of $B_n$;
compute $\tau_n(a) = n/\gcd(n,a)$, the type pair, split count, OR bit and product
residue; tally joint counts; return $H - H(\cdot \mid R)$ for each read-out. Cost
$O(n^2\log n)$; all intermediate data are integers, so the result is a rational
combination of logarithms of tallied integers.

\textbf{C. Certified bracket for a logarithmic constant.} For a target
$V = \sum_i \alpha_i \log_2 m_i$ with rational $\alpha_i$ and a candidate rational
bracket, clear denominators to turn the claim into a comparison of two products of
integer powers, and decide it with one big-integer comparison. This is how every
bracket above is obtained without floating-point arithmetic.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
observation & hidden variable & information \\
\midrule
Frobenius class of a prime & residue degree $T$ & $0.591673$ bits $=H(T)$ \\
quartic character mod $29$ & residue degree $T$ & $0$ bits \\
residue of a semiprime & full type pair & $0.114105$ bits \\
residue of a semiprime & split count & $0.114105$ bits \\
residue of a semiprime & ``at least one splits'' & $0.010306$ bits \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The first line quantifies reciprocity as a lossless channel; the last lines
quantify the arithmetic opacity of products: multiplying two primes destroys about
$80\%$ of the signal, and coarsening the question destroys $90\%$ of what remains.

\begin{conjecture}[second-order decay law]
For prime degrees $q \to \infty$,
$q^2\Isplit(q) = \log_2 q + 2\log_2 e + O(1/q)$ and
$q^2 G(q) \to \log_2 e - 1$.
\end{conjecture}

Numerically $q^2\Isplit(q) - \log_2 q = 2.784,\,2.878,\,2.884,\,2.8845,\,2.8854$
at $q = 7,101,1009,10007,100003$ against $2\log_2 e = 2.885390\ldots$, and
$q^2G(q) = 0.505,\,0.4427,\,0.4427$ at $q = 7,1009,100003$ against
$\log_2 e - 1 = 0.442695\ldots$. Both channels are second-order Taylor remainders
of the same binary entropy, so the leading constants are pure entropy curvature,
carrying no arithmetic information about the field; with the closed forms of
Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} the conjecture reduces to a controlled expansion of
$\log_2(1-1/q)$.

\begin{conjecture}[channel-level primality criterion]
For every $n \ge 2$, $\Ipair(n) = \Isplit(n)$ if and only if $n$ is prime.
\end{conjecture}

The partition-level form is Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}; the ``if'' direction at the
level of channels is Theorem~\ref{thm:suff}; the smallest composite case is
settled with exact values, and exhaustive evaluation for $n \le 12$ shows a
strictly positive gap at every composite $n$. What remains is to convert a
strictly finer partition into a strictly larger mutual information, i.e.\ to show
that the extra type value $n/d$ produced by a divisor $1 < d < n$ survives
conditioning on the product residue.

Further directions include richer observations (several conductors at once),
non-cyclic abelian degrees --- where one expects sufficiency of the split count
exactly for elementary abelian groups of prime exponent --- $k$-almost primes,
where the split count follows $\mathrm{Bin}(k,1/q)$ and one expects
$\Isplit^{(k)}(q) \asymp k^2\log_2 q / q^2$, and effective versions in which
Chebotarev bounds convert these exact channel values into statements about
explicit finite ranges of primes.

\section{Conclusion}

The degree-seven rung is complete and exact: in the septic subfield of
$\Q(\zeta_{29})$ a prime splits completely precisely when its residue lies in
$\{1,12,17,28\}$; the densities are $1/7$ and $6/7$; the type entropy is
$\log_2 7 - \frac67\log_2 6 = 0.5916727\ldots$ bits, attained exactly by the
Frobenius channel, while the quartic character contributes nothing; and none of
this depends on the conductor. For semiprimes the split count follows
$\mathrm{Bin}(2,1/7)$ with cells $(36,12,1)$, the split-count channel is
$0.1141053\ldots$ bits, the OR channel $0.0103060\ldots$ bits, and the latter is
provably never larger. Of the two numerical anchors carried into this round, the
OR figure is confirmed and the split-count figure is corrected. Finally, the
sufficiency of the split count is identified exactly as a primality property of
the degree.

